AU  - Maurya, Ritika; Madan, Sangeeta; Kumari, Monika; Sadhiyan, Bharti
TI  - Biodiversity Loss Due to Climate Change and Pollution
PY  - 2024
AB  - <jats:p>The variety among living things from various environments, such as desert, marine, and terrestrial ecosystems, as well as the ecological complexes they are a part of, is known as biodiversity. The wellbeing of the earth and all of its people depends on the preservation of biodiversity. Human-induced long-term changes in temperatures and weather patterns disrupt the delicate life-supporting equilibrium, causing climate change. Pollution of land, water, and air jeopardizes ecosystems and species globally. According to the World Health Organization, air pollution affects 92% of the population and contributes to 11.6% of global fatalities. Climate change impacts biodiversity through altered breeding and migration behaviors, shifting distribution patterns, invasive species incursions, increased insect attacks, and heightened forest fire risks. India can enhance coordination by enacting eco-friendly laws, integrating these issues into national plans, and adopting biodiversity-based climate change mitigation strategies.</jats:p>
