AU  - Lu, Song; Yue, Ying; Wang, Yihong; Zhang, Dawei; Yang, Biao; Yu, Zhen; Lin, Honghui; Dai, Qiang
TI  - The factors influencing wildlife to use of existing bridges and culverts in Giant Panda National Park
PY  - 2022
AB  - <jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
        <jats:p>Roads, acting as barriers, hamper wildlife movements and disrupt habitat connectivity. Bridges and culverts are common structures on roads, and some of them can function to allow wildlife passage. This study investigated the effects of traffic, surrounding landscape, human disturbance, and bridge and culvert structures on the utilization of bridges and culverts as underpasses by wildlife, using motion-activated infrared camera traps along a 64 km road in Giant Panda National Park, Sichuan, China. The results found that both species richness and counts of wildlife recorded at the bridge and culvert were significantly lower than those observed at the control sites far away from roads. No large-sized wildlife was recorded at the bridges and culverts. Human activities and traffic volume significantly and negatively affected medium-sized wildlife utilization of bridges and culverts. Compared with medium-sized wildlife at control sites, those at bridges and culverts tended to be recorded at night, when traffic was spare. We conclude that functions as wildlife crossing structures served by bridges and culverts were worked but weak. It emphasized the necessity of retrofitting bridges and culverts by mitigation facilities such as noise and light barriers and vegetation restoration on both sides of the roads in Giant Panda National Park.</jats:p>
